        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.026,
            0.845,
            0.044
          ],
          "content": "Chapter 4 — Electricity: Magnetic and Heating Effects"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.893,
            0.03,
            0.917,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": "49"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.083,
            0.497,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": "The magnetic effect of electric current has many practical applications, such as in devices like electromagnets, electric bells, motors, fans, loudspeakers, and more."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.519,
            0.089,
            0.692,
            0.143
          ],
          "content": "Can we use electric current to make a magnet?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.187,
            0.358,
            0.209
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.1.1 Electromagnets"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.234,
            0.482,
            0.258
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 4.2: Let us explore"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.283,
            0.63,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": "z Take around 50 cm long length of a flexible insulated wire, an iron nail, an electric cell, and few iron paper clips."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.345,
            0.629,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "z Tightly wrap the wire around the nail in the form of a coil, as shown in Fig. 4.2, and secure it with an adhesive tape."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.405,
            0.629,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": "z Connect the ends of the wire to the cell. Take care to not connect the wires to the cell for more than a few seconds; otherwise, the cell may weaken quickly."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.638,
            0.29,
            0.919,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.484,
            0.63,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": "z Bring the nail close to the iron paper clips and lift up. Do the clips hang to the ends of the nail?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.687,
            0.501,
            0.855,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 4.2: Coil of wire connected with a cell"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.527,
            0.63,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": "Disconnect the wire from the cell to stop the flow of electric current in the wire. Do the clips fall down?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.588,
            0.743,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": "When electric current flows through the coil, the clips cling to it. But when the current is stopped, the clips no longer cling to it. Let us now try to **investigate** these observations in detail through Activity 4.3."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.681,
            0.537,
            0.705
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 4.3: Let us experiment"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.724,
            0.743,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": "z Take around 100 cm long flexible insulated wire, a piece of chart paper, an iron nail, an electric cell, two magnetic compasses, and few iron/steel paper clips."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.785,
            0.744,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "z Roll a piece of chart paper to make a cylinder of diameter roughly equal to the width of a pencil. Secure it with an adhesive tape."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.846,
            0.743,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": "z Tightly wind around 50 turns of the insulated wire on the cylinder to form a cylindrical coil as shown in Fig. 4.3a. Secure the wire with an adhesive tape."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.77,
            0.733,
            0.901,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.767,
                0.836,
                0.909,
                0.869
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.3: (a) A coil of insulated wire"
            }
